export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>The Fundamentals</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>If you have ever played a PS1 game, you probably have experienced the visual gap between cinematics and gameplay. Pushing past the initial disappointment, you will realize something very clever at play here.</p>\n<p>In terms of game design, the high-quality cinematics were saved for select story moments to highlight their narrative significance, something in-game graphics at the time couldn’t bring forth.</p>\n<p>From the technical standpoint, game developers achieved that by saving video files within the game discs so that when they needed the cinematics, they simply played the existing videos. (And that is the reason why <em>Final Fantasy VIII</em> spanned over 4 CDs.)</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/00cdf1b71a6a3641230f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/00cdf1b71a6a3641230f.webp\" width=\"527\" height=\"297\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Expectation</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c97db182b0b5daac99e5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c97db182b0b5daac99e5.webp\" width=\"527\" height=\"297\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Reality</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/83cc34fa883438e283e4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/83cc34fa883438e283e4.webp\" width=\"529\" height=\"297\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1774c0806d1f345aa65c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1774c0806d1f345aa65c.webp\" width=\"265\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:138px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6728712eb471813dc7fc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6728712eb471813dc7fc.webp\" width=\"265\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:138px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><em>Kingdom Hearts 3</em></p></td><td><p><em>Frozen</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td><p><strong>Pre-rendered vs. Real-time</strong></p>\n<p>Pre-rendered means every frame is generated in advance. Since games are interactive, they have to generate frames as you play them. If games had film-level graphics, gaming devices wouldn’t be able to output frames in real-time.</p>\n<p>Looking at the opposite examples between <em>Kingdom Hearts 3</em> and animated films, you can see how the game’s lighting pales in comparison (among other things). But it is a mandatory sacrifice to get the game running in real-time.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Frame rates</strong></p>\n<p>A frame rate is the amount of frames rendered per second in a game. It is very important for a smooth gameplay experience because it directly affects the responsiveness of the game.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Console</strong> games often run at <strong>30 FPS </strong>or <strong>60 FPS</strong>, sometimes with an option to switch between the two.</li><li><strong>VR</strong> games require at least <strong>90 FPS </strong>to mitigate motion sickness.</li><li>As for <strong>PC</strong> games, they can go as high as the hardware configurations can allow for, <strong>up to 240 FPS</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>When it comes to frame rates, the most important factor is <strong>stability</strong>. A stable frame rate is more desirable than a high but unstable one.</p></td><td><p>Your browser does not support the HTML5 video element<br><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a167679234e634116085.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a167679234e634116085.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"405\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Note the fluctuating frame rate</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b32cde18eea1d73dfee2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b32cde18eea1d73dfee2.webp\" width=\"532\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>A frame in a video game</strong></p>\n<p>Maintaining a consistent frame rate isn’t an easy task. There can be a lot moving parts within a single frame of gameplay. The player character, the non-playable characters, environmental assets, animation, VFX, etc.</p>\n<p>To keep all of these elements on screen without overtaxing the hardware requires a collective effort, from both artists and technical artists. As an artist, if you understand how your assets are rendered, you can make informed decisions when you sculpt and texture them.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Same platform, different results</strong></p>\n<p><em></em>In 2020, they released Ghost of Tsushima, an open-world game taking place on Tsushima Island.</p>\n<p>Their recent title not only has a larger scope but also features other graphical advancements such as greater draw distance and higher polycount. All of which is made possible on the same old PS4.</p>\n<p>The key takeaway is, understanding the ins and outs of your target platform plays a huge role in rendering optimization.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/231ba044f42afb298e01.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/231ba044f42afb298e01.webp\" width=\"525\" height=\"295\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><em>Infamous Second Son</em><em><br></em><em><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f8483f48e64a00635891.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f8483f48e64a00635891.webp\" width=\"525\" height=\"296\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></em><em><br></em><em>Ghost of Tsushima</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/98b51e14fe1bd43e7fd9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/98b51e14fe1bd43e7fd9.webp\" width=\"521\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Same engine, different results</strong></p>\n\n<p>This means, even on the same platform and engine, video game graphics can still differ to varying degrees. So, the more you understand your game engine and target platform, the better.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>In summary</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike films, video games have to generate frame after frame at a steady pace. Each frame in a game, especially in AAA games, has a lot of dynamic elements, which could fluctuate the frame rate. Therefore, game artists have to be conscious about the technical side of game development to strike a balance between art and performance.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e8a7b10d1b62b6317f1a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e8a7b10d1b62b6317f1a.webp\" width=\"596\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/00cdf1b71a6a3641230f.webp", "assets/library-inline/1774c0806d1f345aa65c.webp", "assets/library-inline/231ba044f42afb298e01.webp", "assets/library-inline/6728712eb471813dc7fc.webp", "assets/library-inline/83cc34fa883438e283e4.webp", "assets/library-inline/98b51e14fe1bd43e7fd9.webp", "assets/library-inline/a167679234e634116085.webp", "assets/library-inline/b32cde18eea1d73dfee2.webp", "assets/library-inline/c97db182b0b5daac99e5.webp", "assets/library-inline/e8a7b10d1b62b6317f1a.webp", "assets/library-inline/f8483f48e64a00635891.webp"];
